      <span class="tg-hero__type">Part III of Five</span>
      <h1 class="tg-hero__title">Standing Somewhere: Placement, Assurance, and the Other Side</h1>
      <p class="tg-hero__intro">
        Parts I and II ended on a structural finding: Numbers 16 cannot adjudicate an
        ecclesiological dispute, because the analogy that makes it applicable is itself
        the thing in dispute. That does not mean the question stops mattering. It means
        the question has to be answered on other grounds, and that whoever answers it is
        standing somewhere already. This part asks what a person is supposed to
        <em>do</em> while standing on one side of a divided church.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="tg-hero__key-verse">
        <p>The Lord knows those who are his.</p>
        <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:19">2 Timothy 2:19</a>, quoting <a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:5">Numbers 16:5</a></cite>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
